mexican president andres manuel lopez  obrador has urged crews to intensify  their efforts to rescue 10 miners  trapped underground  families are waiting on the surface for  good news that hasn't come yet  the president thanked the workers and  asked them to redouble their efforts to  be able to find the miners alive we  stress that time is short and that we  need to do what is necessary  for more than four days rescue workers  have been trying to pump water out of  the coal mine which flooded on wednesday  five miners were able to escape but no  contact has been made with the men left  behind  more than 300 soldiers and other  personnel including six military scuba  divers have joined the effort  for those above ground the only thing to  do is pray  we trust in god's mercy and we believe  in miracles and these families have  faith prayer is the only thing that  strengthens us in these times of  distress  doctors are on standby in case of a  breakthrough trying to offer some  comfort to anxious families  in this situation our patients sometimes  don't need medication so much what they  often need is for you to come in tap  them on the shoulder and motivate them  to give them a bit of reassurance in the  midst of all the uncertainty they're  dealing with  a little bit of attention goes a long  way  mexico's main coal producing region has  seen a series of fatal mining accidents  over the years  the worst was in 2006 killing 65 people  only two of the bodies were retrieved  there is still a chance for these tin  miners but with every hour that passes  hopes of finding them alive are fading  leah harding al jazeera
 

